---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
const faqSchema = {
 "@context": "https://schema.org",
 "@type": "FAQPage",
 "mainEntity": [
  {
   "@type": "Question",
   "name": "Is SSL different from TLS?",
   "acceptedAnswer": {
    "@type": "Answer",
    "text": "SSL is the old name (SSL 2.0/3.0, all broken and retired). Modern certificates are 'SSL certificates' colloquially, but the protocol in use is TLS 1.2/1.3."
   }
  },
  {
   "@type": "Question",
   "name": "How long are certificates valid?",
   "acceptedAnswer": {
    "@type": "Answer",
    "text": "Public certificates max out at 398 days (industry policy); Let's Encrypt issues 90-day ones meant for automated renewal."
   }
  },
  {
   "@type": "Question",
   "name": "Does a certificate mean the site is safe?",
   "acceptedAnswer": {
    "@type": "Answer",
    "text": "No: it means the connection is encrypted to THAT domain. Phishing sites get valid certificates too; TLS authenticates the domain, not the operator's honesty."
   }
  },
  {
   "@type": "Question",
   "name": "What is a certificate chain?",
   "acceptedAnswer": {
    "@type": "Answer",
    "text": "Your certificate signed by an intermediate, signed by a root CA pre-trusted in browsers. Missing intermediates cause the classic 'works for me' errors."
   }
  },
  {
   "@type": "Question",
   "name": "Do I need TLS on a site with no forms?",
   "acceptedAnswer": {
    "@type": "Answer",
    "text": "Yes: content integrity, browser warnings, ranking signals, and referral data all depend on it."
   }
  },
  {
   "@type": "Question",
   "name": "What breaks when certificates expire?",
   "acceptedAnswer": {
    "@type": "Answer",
    "text": "Browsers block the page outright. Automated renewal (ACME) exists precisely to prevent this."
   }
  }
 ]
}
---
<BaseLayout
  title="What Is SSL/TLS and Why HTTPS Matters | PromptDNS"
  description="SSL/TLS explained: how certificates and encryption secure web traffic, the handshake process, and why HTTPS is non-negotiable today."
  schema={[faqSchema]}
>
  <div class="mx-auto max-w-3xl px-4 py-12">
    <nav class="mb-4 text-sm text-ink-faint" aria-label="Breadcrumb">
      <a href="/" class="hover:text-accent">Home</a>
       <span aria-hidden="true">/</span>
      <a href="/ssl-tls/" class="hover:text-accent">SSL/TLS Guides</a>
       <span aria-hidden="true">/</span>
      <span class="text-ink-dim">What Is SSL/TLS</span>
    </nav>
    <h1 class="font-display text-3xl font-bold tracking-tight text-ink sm:text-4xl">What Is SSL/TLS and Why HTTPS Matters</h1>
    <p class="mt-5 text-lg leading-relaxed text-ink-dim">SSL is the retired name; TLS is the living protocol. Both refer to the encryption layer that turns HTTP into HTTPS: proving the server's identity with a certificate, then encrypting everything between browser and server so no one on the path can read or alter it.</p>
    <div class="rich-text mt-8">
      <h2 class="font-display mt-10 text-xl font-semibold text-ink">What the handshake does</h2>
      <ol class="list-decimal space-y-1.5 pl-5"><li>The browser connects and proposes TLS versions and ciphers.</li><li>The server presents its certificate: identity + public key, signed by a Certificate Authority the browser already trusts.</li><li>The browser verifies the chain (and may check DNS-level CAA policy was respected at issuance).</li><li>Keys are agreed via ephemeral exchange; all further traffic is encrypted and tamper-evident.</li></ol>
      <h2 class="font-display mt-10 text-xl font-semibold text-ink">What TLS gives you, precisely</h2>
      <ul class="list-disc space-y-1.5 pl-5"><li><strong class="text-ink">Confidentiality:</strong> no one on the network reads your traffic.</li><li><strong class="text-ink">Integrity:</strong> content cannot be modified in transit without detection.</li><li><strong class="text-ink">Authenticity:</strong> the certificate proves you are talking to the real domain.</li></ul><p>What it does not do: confirm the site is honest (a phishing site can have a valid certificate), or hide the domain you are visiting (that is <a href="/doh-vs-dot/">encrypted DNS</a> territory).</p>
      <h2 class="font-display mt-10 text-xl font-semibold text-ink">HTTPS is table stakes</h2>
      <p>Browsers mark plain HTTP as Not Secure, rank it lower, and block its subresources on HTTPS pages. Free certificates (Let’s Encrypt) removed the cost excuse years ago. If any page of yours still serves HTTP, redirect it and enable HSTS; our homepage scanner checks the DNS-side trust signals that complement TLS.</p>
      <h2 class="font-display mt-10 text-xl font-semibold text-ink">Frequently asked questions</h2>
      <div class="mt-3 grid gap-3"><details class="group rounded-xl border border-line bg-panel/60 px-5 py-4 open:border-accent/40"><summary class="flex cursor-pointer list-none items-center justify-between gap-4 font-semibold text-ink [&::-webkit-details-marker]:hidden">Is SSL different from TLS?<span class="text-accent transition-transform duration-200 group-open:rotate-45">+</span></summary><p class="mt-3 text-sm leading-relaxed text-ink-dim">SSL is the old name (SSL 2.0/3.0, all broken and retired). Modern certificates are 'SSL certificates' colloquially, but the protocol in use is TLS 1.2/1.3.</p></details><details class="group rounded-xl border border-line bg-panel/60 px-5 py-4 open:border-accent/40"><summary class="flex cursor-pointer list-none items-center justify-between gap-4 font-semibold text-ink [&::-webkit-details-marker]:hidden">How long are certificates valid?<span class="text-accent transition-transform duration-200 group-open:rotate-45">+</span></summary><p class="mt-3 text-sm leading-relaxed text-ink-dim">Public certificates max out at 398 days (industry policy); Let's Encrypt issues 90-day ones meant for automated renewal.</p></details><details class="group rounded-xl border border-line bg-panel/60 px-5 py-4 open:border-accent/40"><summary class="flex cursor-pointer list-none items-center justify-between gap-4 font-semibold text-ink [&::-webkit-details-marker]:hidden">Does a certificate mean the site is safe?<span class="text-accent transition-transform duration-200 group-open:rotate-45">+</span></summary><p class="mt-3 text-sm leading-relaxed text-ink-dim">No: it means the connection is encrypted to THAT domain. Phishing sites get valid certificates too; TLS authenticates the domain, not the operator's honesty.</p></details><details class="group rounded-xl border border-line bg-panel/60 px-5 py-4 open:border-accent/40"><summary class="flex cursor-pointer list-none items-center justify-between gap-4 font-semibold text-ink [&::-webkit-details-marker]:hidden">What is a certificate chain?<span class="text-accent transition-transform duration-200 group-open:rotate-45">+</span></summary><p class="mt-3 text-sm leading-relaxed text-ink-dim">Your certificate signed by an intermediate, signed by a root CA pre-trusted in browsers. Missing intermediates cause the classic 'works for me' errors.</p></details><details class="group rounded-xl border border-line bg-panel/60 px-5 py-4 open:border-accent/40"><summary class="flex cursor-pointer list-none items-center justify-between gap-4 font-semibold text-ink [&::-webkit-details-marker]:hidden">Do I need TLS on a site with no forms?<span class="text-accent transition-transform duration-200 group-open:rotate-45">+</span></summary><p class="mt-3 text-sm leading-relaxed text-ink-dim">Yes: content integrity, browser warnings, ranking signals, and referral data all depend on it.</p></details><details class="group rounded-xl border border-line bg-panel/60 px-5 py-4 open:border-accent/40"><summary class="flex cursor-pointer list-none items-center justify-between gap-4 font-semibold text-ink [&::-webkit-details-marker]:hidden">What breaks when certificates expire?<span class="text-accent transition-transform duration-200 group-open:rotate-45">+</span></summary><p class="mt-3 text-sm leading-relaxed text-ink-dim">Browsers block the page outright. Automated renewal (ACME) exists precisely to prevent this.</p></details></div>
      <h2 class="font-display mt-10 text-xl font-semibold text-ink">Related</h2>
      <ul class="mt-3 grid gap-2"><li><a href="/caa-record/" class="text-accent hover:text-accent-soft">CAA records →</a></li><li><a href="/what-is-dnssec/" class="text-accent hover:text-accent-soft">Enable DNSSEC →</a></li><li><a href="/what-is-ssl-tls/" class="text-accent hover:text-accent-soft">Free certificates →</a></li></ul>
    </div>
  </div>
</BaseLayout>
